[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'We\'re seeing an emergence of a new class of foundation models specifically designed for automation, which produces only structured outputs really fast and like token by token text generation of a large language model. That means you can directly'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'translate these structured outputs into actions like this model playing Doom. Uh, but it\'s not text generation. And Jeff our system one model from Type-Safe AI is a perfect example of this,'

[00:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which is extremely fast and it\'s very cost-efficient. But now we\'re seeing some open-source alternatives as well. The one that I want to look at is Needle 3. It\'s an automation foundation model for tiny devices. It\'s only uh, 53'

[00:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'million parameter model, which means you can literally run this on a phone. Now, before showing you how it works and walking you through a code notebook, let me show you a quick demo.'

[00:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'In this case, I have a living room with different appliances. And I can produce some complex query like this. I\'m feeling really hot today. Can you turn on the fan?'

[01:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Turn down the temperature to 10° C, and also turn on the bedroom light. Okay, so this was real-time transcription. Now, if we send this, look at this. It actually'

[01:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'turned down the temperature to exactly 10° C that we wanted, only turned on the fan. Now, this whole thing took about 66 milliseconds.'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But I want you to focus on this structured output that the model generated. And if you look at this, this looks like function call, where the model is simply generating JSON schema based on unstructured data that is'

[01:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'coming in. Okay, so in the rest of the video, I want to talk about how exactly they\'re doing it, and then I\'ll show you a code example, and also some caveats where you need to be careful on using this model. So, this is just 35 MB. If you compare'

[01:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it with something like a 7B model that is about 4 GB in 4-bit, it\'s actually smaller than Whisper small,'

[02:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but it\'s specifically tuned for function calling. And it can do two function calls under 97 milliseconds on a CPU. You don\'t even need GPU to run this. Now, this is a third model from a'

[02:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'company called Cactus, and their whole focus has been building these powerful but tiny models for automation or function calling. Okay, but you might be thinking you can'

[02:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually do exactly the same thing with uh normal large language model, and that is true. Uh but the problem is a large language model is going to generate text one token at a time. It is going to also'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have uh text description, and you need a parser to parse that JSON schema. But, Needle 3 is specifically designed to generate these structured outputs directly from your raw unstructured text'

[03:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'input. You don\'t need a parser on top of it. Now, in the demo that I showed you, it takes this unstructured string, and based on the function definitions'

[03:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that you have, which we\'re going to look at in a minute, it simply extracts the most relevant inputs and set them in your schema. That means it cannot hallucinate just like the'

[03:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'System 1 Jeff model from TypeChat. And it can directly validate the schema and the expected input values. Okay, so in terms of architecture, they\'re calling it simple attention'

[03:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'network. So, in a standard transformer, they remove the feed forward network and simply replace that with a multi-layer perceptron with a total of 225.6'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thousand parameters. Along with an n-gram, which can actually give you embedding if you\'re looking up something directly. So, it\'s substantially smaller compared to a standard transformer model. But,'

[04:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here\'s the beauty. Even though it\'s a 20 layers model, but each one of them or a combination of them can be used as a separate model. So, for example,'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can slice it in any way you want, and that will become your network. So, if you\'re using just the two layers, that\'s going to be 13 megabytes, which is perfect to be run on something like a modern phone. Or, you can use the'

[04:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'whole 20 layers. Still, it\'s just a 35 megabyte file, which is incredible. But again, this is specifically designed for automation. It cannot do anything'

[04:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
